#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130189} :activity/id #uuid "2beb370d-eb6c-515e-b476-342e218e4078" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048435 :topic/id #uuid "9f05a2f3-96ce-5d8f-a6a0-1494c05fed7e" :topic/math-academy-id 1986} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129195 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129023 :question/id #uuid "97cc7a63-900d-52e8-ad0b-2232309d6335" :question/math-academy-id "e-780"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f945ef94-171c-5c19-adb3-f496408dc31b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050639}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235443}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Limit at Infinity of a Cube Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "45453d10-c140-58f5-80ac-1332423109ce" :step/math-academy-id 2666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130189 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130324 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nbelow. We will use this graph to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 4} \\sqrt{x} \\,$.\n\n![](images/e6/e6423b96ce60348e780ffd437207bdbeb456e3aae2888d7525f8c4e680f4ee43.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $4$, the value of $y$ approaches $\\sqrt{4}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} \\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nThe limit of the square root function at a point is equal to the value of the square root function at that point, and this is true for any point *that is greater than $0$.* However, for points that are less than or equal to $0$, the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\nlim_(x → c)\\sqrt{x} = {\\sqrt{c}\\begin{vmatrix}ifc > 0 \\\\ DNE\\end{vmatrix}ifc \\le 0\n$$\n\nWhy doesn't the limit exist for\n\n$$\nc \\leq 0\n$$\n\n?\n\n- For $c < 0$, the limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, c} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because the square root function $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative inputs.\n- Although $\\sqrt{x}$ is defined at $x=0$, the left limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0^-} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because $\\sqrt{x}$ is undefined left of $x=0$. Consequently, the overall limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist either." :tutorial/id #uuid "065200d4-2dec-598b-bc96-23cbca11d178" :tutorial/math-academy-id 214 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9659c87c-7d2f-5bc9-bfcf-1654a3f32b47" :step/math-academy-id 392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130190}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130190 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129256 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128928 :question/id #uuid "57abae04-4bc2-5fb4-a5b0-5c0c6c85f955" :question/math-academy-id "e-49"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3eae4ea-fb38-5efa-843e-783327b82702" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050987}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233766}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Limit of a Square Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "b4d1a1fc-a4d5-59ee-a182-8166e3835860" :step/math-academy-id 393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130193}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130325 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our plot of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt x\n$$\n\nand work out its limits at infinity.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b7cbc735c20a866b1d22287e2f3a1ffc247931c2de08c545cbd2fe922397b.png)\n\nAs $x \\to \\infty$, the square root function increases without bound. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\sqrt{x} = \\infty\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, when computing the limit as $x \\to -\\infty$, remember that the square root function isn't defined for $x<0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty}\\sqrt{x} = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dbe32211-a583-5d8f-a17d-6df81655eadc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 216 :tutorial/title "The Limits at Infinity of the Square Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "51417fe4-26a7-5030-ba58-abf67d0e60d4" :step/math-academy-id 396 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130194}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130194 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129258 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128929 :question/id #uuid "1b850386-4730-512a-8408-932f7b6931b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-50"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad10ca6b-d130-559b-a201-15127659de13" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234517}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity of a Square Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "2dd140f5-c332-59c2-b2fd-408d9ab8c5c6" :step/math-academy-id 397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130197}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130197 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130326 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of the cube root function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nbelow.\n\n![](images/20/20deb13b6bce6608c84db84472e19ade1d073c9562f4c414ebee36f4f9e9aebc.png)\n\nUnlike the square root function, the cube root is defined for all real $x$. As $x$ approaches any finite number $a$, the limit of the cube root function can be obtained by simply substituting $x=a$ into the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\sqrt[3]{x} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we can see from the graph that the limits at infinity are\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x} = \\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x} = -\\infty\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34c1d017-afd0-500a-8f4b-9486d550e2e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 218 :tutorial/title "Limits of Cube Root Functions"} :step/id #uuid "241dca57-2db4-539d-8844-54cde45b427c" :step/math-academy-id 399 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130198}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130198 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128932 :question/id #uuid "fbac92a0-de6c-556a-8bf1-030401a5df36" :question/math-academy-id "e-51"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631dd0e-a893-5099-8b52-e7a543e7f783" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234520}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Limit of a Cube Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "df25e7f2-a199-5459-8a8c-495ade542985" :step/math-academy-id 400 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130079}}] :activity/title "Limits of Radical Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129334}]}
